WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:02.735
SAM SHERIDAN: This
is a place that has

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:04.604
seen a lot of human tragedy.

00:00:04.671 --> 00:00:07.340
You can bundle it up under
the blanket of a curse,

00:00:07.407 --> 00:00:09.743
but you can't deny that
there is something at work

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:13.680
here, some relationship
between West Virginian industry

00:00:13.747 --> 00:00:17.917
and a seemingly endless
cycle of calamity.

00:00:17.984 --> 00:00:21.421
And since Austin Caperton
won't be providing any answers,

00:00:21.488 --> 00:00:25.491
I'm turning to a
trusted old buddy--

00:00:25.558 --> 00:00:26.292
science.

00:00:29.295 --> 00:00:33.767
So what we do in this
lab is we build stuff.

00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:36.635
And we break it to figure
out how to make it stronger.

00:00:36.703 --> 00:00:39.005
What a cool job, man.

00:00:39.072 --> 00:00:41.808
SAM SHERIDAN: No disaster is
woven into the local folklore

00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:45.612
of West Virginia quite like the
collapse of the Silver Bridge.

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:47.446
I think there's
reason behind it.

00:00:47.514 --> 00:00:48.381
Yeah.

00:00:48.448 --> 00:00:50.550
It just didn't
happen to happen.

00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:53.285
They said that the
Moth Man appeared

00:00:53.353 --> 00:00:55.655
before the bridge fell.

00:00:55.722 --> 00:00:58.324
It's like a legend.

00:00:58.391 --> 00:01:00.393
SAM SHERIDAN: Although I'm
fairly certain a flying cryptid

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:04.230
didn't cause this disaster,
the Silver Bridge did collapse

00:01:04.297 --> 00:01:06.765
at the exact spot where the
Battle of Point Pleasant

00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:08.033
was fought.

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:09.669
And some say that was
the cause of Chief

00:01:09.736 --> 00:01:12.238
Cornstalk's dying curse.

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:14.073
So what does science
have to say about

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:15.875
that peculiar coincidence?

00:01:15.942 --> 00:01:18.178
MATT HEBOON: This is a
model of the Silver Bridge.

00:01:18.244 --> 00:01:19.579
SAM SHERIDAN: Cool.

00:01:19.646 --> 00:01:22.048
So the Silver Bridge was
similar to a suspension

00:01:22.115 --> 00:01:24.750
bridge except that
the suspension

00:01:24.818 --> 00:01:27.253
cables were actually eyebars.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:30.256
So tell me exactly how
the Silver Bridge failed.

00:01:30.323 --> 00:01:31.291
What happened?

00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:32.692
There was a flaw.

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:34.594
It was actually located
at the end of the eyebar.

00:01:34.661 --> 00:01:36.463
That flaw was extremely
small, and actually

00:01:36.529 --> 00:01:38.298
wasn't visible
because it was hidden

00:01:38.364 --> 00:01:40.433
under the cap of the pin.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:45.705
And in this case, the very
small flaw initiated a fracture.

00:01:45.772 --> 00:01:47.740
Can you show me what
that would look like?

00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:48.541
Yeah.

00:01:51.344 --> 00:01:53.479
MATT HEBOON: OK, so we're
going to apply a tension lug,

00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:56.516
meaning we're going
to pull on this.

00:01:56.583 --> 00:02:00.119
[creaking]

00:02:00.186 --> 00:02:00.920
What's this kip?

00:02:00.987 --> 00:02:01.887
What is that.

00:02:01.955 --> 00:02:03.223
So a kip is 1,000 pounds.

00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:06.125
So we're at 0.21
thousand pounds,

00:02:06.192 --> 00:02:08.026
or in other words, 22 pounds.

00:02:08.094 --> 00:02:10.697
It's incrementally adding load.

00:02:10.763 --> 00:02:13.266
This no longer is
contributing at all.

00:02:13.333 --> 00:02:15.001
All the force that
was in this component

00:02:15.068 --> 00:02:16.336
is now dumped into this one.

00:02:16.402 --> 00:02:18.004
SAM SHERIDAN: Right.

00:02:18.071 --> 00:02:21.674
In the case of the Silver
Bridge, this happened so fast.

00:02:21.741 --> 00:02:23.810
The first one failed
and immediately--

00:02:23.877 --> 00:02:24.611
[snap]

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.482
Let's-- let's do
it on our model.

00:02:29.549 --> 00:02:32.151
MATT HEBOON: OK.

00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:33.386
SAM SHERIDAN: And
here's where we smash

00:02:33.453 --> 00:02:36.856
stuff in the name of science.

00:02:36.923 --> 00:02:40.260
So we're going to simulate
the fracture of the eyebar

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:42.762
by cutting one of
the yellow strings.

00:02:42.829 --> 00:02:49.569
[dramatic music] When
one eyebar failed,

00:02:49.636 --> 00:02:51.571
there just wasn't enough
capacity in the rest

00:02:51.638 --> 00:02:55.508
of the system to resist all of
the weight of the structure,

00:02:55.575 --> 00:02:58.378
and therefore the
entire bridge collapsed.

00:03:05.151 --> 00:03:07.787
SAM SHERIDAN: So the
Silver Bridge collapses.

00:03:07.854 --> 00:03:10.823
What's the way this could
have been prevented?

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.760
MATT HEBOON: The original
designs for this bridge

00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:15.762
called for a suspension cable.

00:03:15.828 --> 00:03:19.332
But they decided to opt
for a less redundant system

00:03:19.399 --> 00:03:22.869
with the eyebar chains, which
allowed them to dramatically

00:03:22.936 --> 00:03:24.470
reduce the cost.

00:03:24.537 --> 00:03:28.708
But it also dramatically reduced
the redundancy in the system.

00:03:28.775 --> 00:03:30.310
SAM SHERIDAN: For
those of you at home

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:32.445
who never took an interest
in civil engineering,

00:03:32.512 --> 00:03:33.947
let me spell it out for you.

00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:36.849
Redundancy equals safety.

00:03:36.916 --> 00:03:39.686
So if they hadn't done
this with the lowest bidder,

00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:42.422
there's a possibility that that
failure would have happened,

00:03:42.488 --> 00:03:45.692
but they would have had enough
redundancy that the bridge

00:03:45.758 --> 00:03:46.626
wouldn't have collapsed.

00:03:46.693 --> 00:03:48.394
That's right.

